@anon.ecbbdf4ce99fb387dde54de6349d4c38.951 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [3730 x i8] }> <{ [3730 x i8] c"Typst has special [syntax]($syntax/#math) and library functions to typeset\0Amathematical formulas. Math formulas can be displayed inline with text or as\0Aseparate blocks. They will be typeset into their own block if they start and\0Aend with at least one space (e.g. `[$ x^2 $]`).\0A\0A# Variables\0AIn math, single letters are always displayed as is. Multiple letters,\0Ahowever, are interpreted as variables and functions. To display multiple\0Aletters verbatim, you can place them into quotes and to access single letter\0Avariables, you can use the [hash syntax]($scripting/#expressions).\0A\0A```example\0A$ A = pi r^2 $\0A$ \22area\22 = pi dot \22radius\22^2 $\0A$ cal(A) :=\0A    { x in RR | x \22is natural\22 } $\0A#let x = 5\0A$ #x < 17 $\0A```\0A\0A# Symbols\0AMath mode makes a wide selection of [symbols]($category/symbols/sym) like\0A`pi`, `dot`, or `RR` available. Many mathematical symbols are available in\0Adifferent variants. You can select between different variants by applying\0A[modifiers]($symbol) to the symbol. Typst further recognizes a number of\0Ashorthand sequences like `=>` that approximate a symbol. When such a\0Ashorthand exists, the symbol's documentation lists it.\0A\0A```example\0A$ x < y => x gt.eq.not y $\0A```\0A\0A# Line Breaks\0AFormulas can also contain line breaks. Each line can contain one or multiple\0A_alignment points_ (`&`) which are then aligned.\0A\0A```example\0A$ sum_(k=0)^n k\0A    &= 1 + ... + n \\\0A    &= (n(n+1)) / 2 $\0A```\0A\0A# Function calls\0AMath mode supports special function calls without the hash prefix. In these\0A\22math calls\22, the argument list works a little differently than in code:\0A\0A- Within them, Typst is still in \22math mode\22. Thus, you can write math\0A  directly into them, but need to use hash syntax to pass code expressions\0A  (except for strings, which are available in the math syntax).\0A- They support positional and named arguments, but don't support trailing\0A  content blocks and argument spreading.\0A- They provide additional syntax for 2-dimensional argument lists. The\0A  semicolon (`;`) merges preceding arguments separated by commas into an\0A  array argument.\0A\0A```example\0A$ frac(a^2, 2) $\0A$ vec(1, 2, delim: \22[\22) $\0A$ mat(1, 2; 3, 4) $\0A$ lim_x =\0A    op(\22lim\22, limits: #true)_x $\0A```\0A\0ATo write a verbatim comma or semicolon in a math call, escape it with a\0Abackslash. The colon on the other hand is only recognized in a special way\0Aif directly preceded by an identifier, so to display it verbatim in those\0Acases, you can just insert a space before it.\0A\0AFunctions calls preceded by a hash are normal code function calls and not\0Aaffected by these rules.\0A\0A# Alignment\0AWhen equations include multiple _alignment points_ (`&`), this creates\0Ablocks of alternatingly right- and left-aligned columns. In the example\0Abelow, the expression `(3x + y) / 7` is right-aligned and `= 9` is\0Aleft-aligned. The word \22given\22 is also left-aligned because `&&` creates two\0Aalignment points in a row, alternating the alignment twice. `& &` and `&&`\0Abehave exactly the same way. Meanwhile, \22multiply by 7\22 is right-aligned\0Abecause just one `&` precedes it. Each alignment point simply alternates\0Abetween right-aligned/left-aligned.\0A\0A```example\0A$ (3x + y) / 7 &= 9 && \22given\22 \\\0A  3x + y &= 63 & \22multiply by 7\22 \\\0A  3x &= 63 - y && \22subtract y\22 \\\0A  x &= 21 - y/3 & \22divide by 3\22 $\0A```\0A\0A# Math fonts\0AYou can set the math font by with a [show-set rule]($styling/#show-rules) as\0Ademonstrated below. Note that only special OpenType math fonts are suitable\0Afor typesetting maths.\0A\0A```example\0A#show math.equation: set text(font: \22Fira Math\22)\0A$ sum_(i in NN) 1 + i $\0A```\0A\0A# Math module\0AAll math functions are part of the `math` [module]($scripting/#modules),\0Awhich is available by default in equations. Outside of equations, they can\0Abe accessed with the `math.` prefix." }>, align 1
